WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.810
There's an old saying that advises performers
to never work with children or animals.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.920
After all, they both lack the self-control
of adult humans, and if they don't feel like

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.240
working, they just won't.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.730
Here are some films in which animals wreaked
havoc while the cameras were rolling.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.610
Mr. Popper's Penguins

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:21.369
Jim Carrey loves to goof around on the set,
riffing on lines, making outrages sounds,

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:22.369
and teasing co-stars.

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:25.189
"Scooba-dee-ba-da-bop bop bop.

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:26.189
Honk.

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:27.189
Honk.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:28.180
Honk.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.100
Honk!"

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.340
It's all part of the Jim Carrey package.

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:36.339
But if he messes around a bit too long, not
all of Mr. Popper's penguins are gonna put

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:37.339
up with it.

00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:40.139
"I'll introduce you, somebody wants to talk."

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.380
"Wanna say 'hi'?"

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.280
"Ohh yeah!"

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.800
"Go this way...

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.160
OK.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.400
Know what?

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.080
Sometime we need a little… hey, come here,
come here, no!"

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:54.089
Outlander

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:59.809
This historical TV series is full of serious,
dramatic moments set on the battlefields of

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:01.039
18th-century Scotland.

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:05.399
But behind the scenes, certain actors march
to beat of their own drum — and go by their

00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:06.399
own schedule.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.280
Take for example, the horse who didn't feel
like waiting until the scene was finished

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.119
to take care of personal business.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.280
"Oh yes, quite enough."

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.040
The Lone Ranger

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:23.020
Disney's attempt to reboot The Lone Ranger
was also an attempt to revive the old-school

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.100
big-screen Western.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:29.070
As a result, Armie Hammer and Johnny Depp
spend large portions of the movie on horseback.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.990
All that time together led to what looks like
an on-set romance…or at least a crush.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.860
"I love you..."

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.760
"You have traveled far."

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.160
"Okay don't come, see if I care."

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.180
"Marker, marker!"

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:50.179
Anchorman

00:01:50.179 --> 00:01:54.409
In the ridiculous classic Anchorman, Will
Ferrell's character Ron Burgundy winds up

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:55.871
a shell of a man located in, well,

00:01:55.871 --> 00:01:59.829
"I'm in a glass case of emotion!"

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:03.439
And it's all because he threw a burrito out
the window and hit a biker, who responded

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:06.720
by punting Ron's beloved dog, Baxter, off
a bridge.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.069
But behind the scenes, it appears the "actor"
playing Baxter could sense the danger ahead.

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:13.720
"Cuz I will give you a rap on the Jack Johnson!

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.950
You hear me?!"

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:15.950
"Yep.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:16.960
Now this is going to happen!"

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:21.420
"Yep, he doesn't like you."

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.069
Anchorman 2

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:27.680
The beginning of Anchorman 2 shows Ron getting
the old news team from Anchorman back together.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.260
He finds Paul Rudd's character, ace reporter
Brian Fantana, working as a highly esteemed

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.620
kitten photographer.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.040
But some on-the-set tension between the "models"
may have led to some, well, cat fights.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.320
"Ohhhh!"

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.220
The Interview

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.841
German Shepherds are one of the most fearsome
breeds of dog, liable to maim, murder, and

00:02:48.841 --> 00:02:50.630
dismember if they're trained that way.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.019
Or, apparently, that rage can be re-directed.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:57.180
Unfortunately for Seth Rogen in The Interview,
the scenes he shot with an extremely friendly

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.680
German Shepherd also required nudity.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.000
The 
Hangover

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.360
Dogs are essentially professional people-lickers.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:17.750
While filming The Hangover, Zach Galifianakis
established a very special bond with a dog

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.549
named Tucker, although, it doesn't seem like
it was always the dog who was doing the licking.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:23.980
"I love you so much!"

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.800
"I've got so much peanut butter in my beard
right now."

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.420
The Proposal

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.350
You'd think acting opposite Oscar-winning
movie star and America's sweetheart Sandra

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.280
Bullock would get actors to behave professionally
and keep a laser focus on their scene partner.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.680
Apparently, not all actors.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.480
"Come on ya little…"

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.980
Late Night with Conan O'Brien

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:57.489
During a taping of an episode of his old NBC
show in 2007, Conan O'Brien brought out a

00:03:57.489 --> 00:03:59.590
duck, without a plan as to what to do with
it.

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:00.780
Well, the duck had his own idea.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.080
The 
bit was so well-received that O'Brien brought

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.939
the duck back, naming him "Quackers, the s---eating
duck."

